\section{Introduction}
The California condor is a large scavenger native to the southwestern United States, identified by its $9\frac{1}{2}$ foot wingspan and bald pink head. Scavenging birds such as these play a critical part in their native ecosystems, preventing the spread of disease by disposing of carrion before deadly bacteria are exposed to the environment. Despite their presence in the United States for thousands of years, the introduction of the insecticide DDT in the $20^{\text{th}}$ century caused their numbers to plummet to the tens by the 1980's. To prevent extinction, all 22 of the estimated birds remaining in the wild were captured and allowed to breed in captivity, where their numbers were gradually able to increase enough to begin reintroduction back into their native mountain ranges.

In the wild, California condors breed slowly and deliberately. After reaching sexual maturity at around 8 years of age, a mating pair will produce a single egg that is incubated for around 6 months. After hatching, it will be dependent on its parents for another year. Even after leaving its parents, wild condors still face many natural and man-made threats, leading to high rates of mortality \cite{ref_condor_demographics}. In captivity however, researchers have been able to increase the productivity rate of mating pairs through a process called double-clutching. During this process, the first egg produced by a mating pair is taken and cared for by researchers before being introduced to its natural habitat. Without viable offspring, the mating pair will often try again, doubling the number of young produced in a year.

In this paper, we create a system dynamics model of changing condor populations in the wild and in captivity with and without double-clutching. Our model takes into account condor age structure, productivity, mortality, and the carrying capacities of captive and wild environments. In developing this model, we examine the viability of repopulation efforts under varying mortality and release conditions.

\section{Methodology}

\subsection{Numerical Integration Methods}
The demographic system is evaluated using the Forward Euler numerical integration technique with a discrete step size of $\Delta t = 1\text{ yr}$. For any stage variable governed by $\frac{dY}{dt} = \text{Inflow} - \text{Outflow}$, the value at the subsequent annual increment is computed as:
\begin{equation}
Y(t + \Delta t) = Y(t) + \Delta t \left(\text{Inflow}(t) - \text{Outflow}(t)\right)
\end{equation}
Because biological cohorts graduate between single-year age brackets annually, an integer time step of $\Delta t = 1$ ensures individual cohorts progress systematically across successive stages.

\subsection{Captive Subpopulation Dynamics}
Captive condor pairs operating under double-clutching achieve a fecundity rate of $f_{\text{cap}} = 1.8\text{ yr}^{-1}$, corresponding to an individual rate of $m_{\text{cap}} = 0.9\text{ yr}^{-1}$ assuming an equal sex ratio. Captive birds reach reproductive maturity at age 7 ($Y_{A,\text{cap}}$).

The differential equations governing each captive age cohort are:
\begin{align}
\frac{dY_{0,\text{cap}}}{dt} &= m_{\text{cap}} Y_{A,\text{cap}}(t) - Y_{0,\text{cap}}(t) \\
\frac{dY_{1,\text{cap}}}{dt} &= (1 - d_0) Y_{0,\text{cap}}(t) - Y_{1,\text{cap}}(t) \\
\frac{dY_{2,\text{cap}}}{dt} &= (1 - r(t))(1 - d_1) Y_{1,\text{cap}}(t) - Y_{2,\text{cap}}(t) \\
\frac{dY_{k,\text{cap}}}{dt} &= (1 - d_I) Y_{k-1,\text{cap}}(t) - Y_{k,\text{cap}}(t) \quad \text{for } k = 3, 4, 5, 6 \\
\frac{dY_{A,\text{cap}}}{dt} &= (1 - d_I) Y_{6,\text{cap}}(t) - d_A Y_{A,\text{cap}}(t)
\end{align}

The release fraction $r(t)$ controls juvenile transfers to the wild. If total captive occupancy exceeds the facility limit of 150 birds, all Year 1 juveniles are released:
\begin{equation}
r(t) = \begin{cases} 
0.80, & \text{if } N_{\text{cap}}(t) \le 150 \\
1.00, & \text{if } N_{\text{cap}}(t) > 150 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $N_{\text{cap}}(t) = \sum_{k=0}^{6} Y_{k,\text{cap}}(t) + Y_{A,\text{cap}}(t)$.

\subsection{Wild Subpopulation Dynamics and Reintroduction}
Wild condors reproduce at a base fecundity of $f_{\text{wild}} = 0.3729\text{ yr}^{-1}$ per pair ($m_{\text{wild}} = 0.18645\text{ yr}^{-1}$ per adult) and reach breeding maturity at age 8 ($Y_{A,\text{wild}}$). Natural reproduction is subject to an environmental carrying capacity of 400 birds:
\begin{align}
\frac{dY_{0,\text{wild}}}{dt} &= m_{\text{wild}} Y_{A,\text{wild}}(t)\left(1 - \frac{N_{\text{wild}}(t)}{400}\right) - Y_{0,\text{wild}}(t) \\
\frac{dY_{1,\text{wild}}}{dt} &= (1 - d_0) Y_{0,\text{wild}}(t) - Y_{1,\text{wild}}(t)
\end{align}
where $N_{\text{wild}}(t) = \sum_{k=0}^{7} Y_{k,\text{wild}}(t) + Y_{A,\text{wild}}(t)$.

The wild 2-year-old cohort ($Y_{2,\text{wild}}$) receives both native Year 1 survivors and the released captive Year 1 cohort:
\begin{align}
\frac{dY_{2,\text{wild}}}{dt} &= (1 - d_1) Y_{1,\text{wild}}(t) + r(t)(1 - d_1) Y_{1,\text{cap}}(t) - Y_{2,\text{wild}}(t) \\
\frac{dY_{k,\text{wild}}}{dt} &= (1 - d_I) Y_{k-1,\text{wild}}(t) - Y_{k,\text{wild}}(t) \quad \text{for } k = 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 \\
\frac{dY_{A,\text{wild}}}{dt} &= (1 - d_I) Y_{7,\text{wild}}(t) - d_A Y_{A,\text{wild}}(t)
\end{align}

